"hook": "5′ → 3′ always, and the enzymes at the fork in order.",
"formulas": [],
"vars": [],
"eli5": "DNA is a zipper. Helicase unzips it, and polymerase builds a new half on each old half. Polymerase can only add to the 3′ end, so one new strand (leading) is built smoothly toward the fork, and the other (lagging) is built backward in short pieces (Okazaki fragments) that get glued together.",
"points": [
"Central dogma: DNA → RNA (transcription) → protein (translation). Retroviruses use reverse transcriptase: RNA → DNA.",
"Replication is semiconservative: each new double helix = 1 old strand + 1 new strand.",
"New strands grow 5′ → 3′; the template is read 3′ → 5′.",
{
"t": "Enzymes at the fork, in order:",
"sub": [
"Helicase unwinds → single-strand binding proteins (SSB) keep strands apart → topoisomerase (gyrase) relieves supercoiling ahead.",
"Primase lays an RNA primer → DNA polymerase III (prokaryotes) or δ/ε (eukaryotes) extends from the primer's 3′-OH.",
"DNA polymerase I (prokaryotes) or RNase H (eukaryotes) removes primers → DNA ligase seals the gaps."
]
},
"Leading strand: continuous, toward the fork. Lagging strand: Okazaki fragments, away from the fork.",
"Telomerase extends chromosome ends using its own RNA template (active in stem and cancer cells).",
"A pairs with T by **2** H-bonds; G pairs with C by **3**, so GC-rich DNA melts at a higher temperature.",
"Prokaryotes: one origin, circular DNA. Eukaryotes: many origins, linear chromosomes."
],
"traps": [
"DNA polymerase cannot start from scratch; it needs a primer. RNA polymerase does not.",
"Polymerase proofreads with 3′ → 5′ exonuclease activity, even though it builds 5′ → 3′."
],
"mnemonics": [
"Purines (two rings): **\"Pure As Gold\"** (A, G). Pyrimidines: **\"CUT the Py\"** (C, U, T).",
"Fork order: **\"Hungry Sharks Took Pretty Pink Lollipops\"**: Helicase, SSB, Topoisomerase, Primase, Polymerase, Ligase.",
"**\"Leading leads, lagging lags\"**: smooth vs choppy."
],
